  "comments": "<p><a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/5c50a57c61100.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/5c50a57c61100.jpg\" alt=\"\"></a><br><br>Die clash can be oriented in other ways, with different parts of either die occurring in different areas of the coin. Sometimes, multiple clashes on the same side. See images below, where both the obverse and reverse have multiple die clashes: &nbsp;</p><figure class=\"image\"><a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/683f72377bef5.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/683f72377bef5.jpg\"></a><figcaption>Three instances of ‘VOS’, with the top instance being a double strike.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"image\"><a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/683f723365ca0.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/683f723365ca0.jpg\"></a><figcaption>Obverse can also have die clashes</figcaption></figure><p>The directive from the Central Bank of Brazil (BCB) which requested the creation of the coin stated the figure were to be stamped on the obverse of the coin. Numista, however, to keep order in its database prefers to show the figure as stamped on the reverse.</p>",
